WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:05.240
With the release of Guardians of the Galaxy
Vol. 2, Marvel Studios has cemented its ability

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:09.550
to take pretty much any random character in
its vast publishing history and turn it into

00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:11.050
a blockbuster event.

00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:14.620
While it's not surprising that the second
movie starring the Guardians was a hit, the

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:18.380
movie's end did raise some interesting questions
— both within the Marvel Cinematic Universe,

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:21.750
as well as our own, much more boring, terrifying
reality.

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:23.079
What kinds of questions?

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:24.570
These questions, dummy!

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:25.940
"What are you doing?"

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:29.420
"Dance off bro, me and you!"

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:36.380
Oh, and, before we get into the real nitty-gritty,
just remember: there are obviously spoilers

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:37.380
ahead.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:38.700
Ego on Earth

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:43.040
When Star-Lord's dad, Ego the Living Planet,
enacted his scheme to take over the universe

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:47.750
with big, blobby versions of himself, we got
a glimpse of total chaos erupting in Missouri

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:48.750
on Earth.

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:53.510
What we didn't see, however, was the response
of any of Earth's many, many superpowered

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:54.510
people.

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:58.990
Scarlet Witch, Hawkeye, the Vision — none
of the Avengers were anywhere to be found.

00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:02.190
Even Ant-Man couldn't apparently be bothered
to put in an appearance.

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:05.920
Unless he was there, but was just, like, too
small for us to see…

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:07.970
Anyway, let's get back on topic.

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:11.909
The lack of a superheroic response on Earth
was conspicuous by its absence.

00:01:11.909 --> 00:01:17.040
So when, if ever, will we follow up on the
giant blob-splosion that occurred in Missouri

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:19.430
as a result of Quills' crappy dad?

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:23.550
Probably not in Thor: Ragnarok, which seems
to take place almost exclusively away from

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:24.550
Earth.

00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:28.930
Will Peter Parker flip past news of the disaster
while he's watching TV in Spider-Man: Homecoming?

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:32.630
We know that the Guardians of the Galaxy will
have some sort of presence in the upcoming

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:37.680
Avengers: Infinity War, so dealing with the
Ego-blob's aftermath may give the spacefaring

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:40.250
heroes the excuse to get involved with events
on Earth.

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:42.710
And while we're on that subject…

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:44.080
Nebula on the hunt

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:49.030
By the end of the film, Gamora's sister Nebula
is essentially a member of the Guardians,

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:51.370
even if no one really likes her very much.

00:01:51.370 --> 00:01:56.070
But she still leaves to punish Thanos for
putting her through hell her entire life.

00:01:56.070 --> 00:02:00.369
Actress Karen Gillan has already confirmed
that she'll appear in Infinity War, so she

00:02:00.369 --> 00:02:04.880
must be colliding with Thanos one way or another
once that movie debuts in 2018.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:09.229
And that's yet another potential way for the
Guardians to get mixed up with the rest of

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:11.340
Earth's mightiest heroes.

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:12.340
Star-Lord's status

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:17.450
We'd had hints that Star-Lord had some pretty
heavy-duty DNA back in the first Guardians

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:18.530
of the Galaxy.

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:22.170
There have been plenty of little ones, but
the biggie was when he held onto the Infinity

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:27.260
Stone to defeat Ronan the Accuser and somehow
didn't explode into a million star-bits.

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:31.620
Ego made it sound like the only thing keeping
hi m immortal was the living planet's glowing,

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:32.870
brainy core.

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:34.920
But is that, y'know … true?

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:38.950
Ego said that none of his other children had
managed to retain their Celestial powers except

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:39.950
for Peter.

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:43.880
So that means Ego would have no idea how the
powers of one of his children would actually

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:46.080
work after his own death, right?

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:49.410
Moreover, why would Peter lose his powers
just because his deadbeat dad melted into

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:50.410
space sand?

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:54.660
If you get your dad's blue eyes, they don't
fall out of your head after he kicks the bucket.

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:58.980
It's entirely possible that Quill is still
every bit the Star-Lord he was before Ego

00:02:58.980 --> 00:02:59.980
died.

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:04.390
On the other hand, if Ego is right, that opens
up a whole different can of space-worms.

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:07.340
Peter Quill has spent his whole life being,
well, him.

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:11.750
But if he's relied on his Celestial powers
to get by in life up to this point, how will

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:15.190
the sudden loss of those powers affect him
going forward?

00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:16.330
Romance?

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:20.830
By the end of the movie, we saw Gamora finally
hint that she may have the same feelings for

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:22.790
Star-Lord that he has for her.

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:27.370
But beyond that, where the relationship between
these two characters goes is anybody's guess.

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:32.590
Conflict drives stories, and, as Peter pointed
out when he referenced Sam and Diane on Cheers,

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:36.209
having the couple get together is usually
where the story — and the audience's interest

00:03:36.209 --> 00:03:38.460
in said relationship — ends.

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:41.980
Don't be surprised to see that there's actually
trouble in paradise between the two when they

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:42.989
next appear.

00:03:42.989 --> 00:03:46.050
And maybe someone else could come into the
picture and throw a monkey wrench into the

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:47.050
relationship.

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:52.570
Like, say, Starfox, the legendary space Lothario
and younger brother to a certain purple-faced

00:03:52.570 --> 00:03:54.250
jerk named Thanos.

00:03:54.250 --> 00:03:56.190
And while we're on the subject of romance…

00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:57.890
No heroes necessary

00:03:57.890 --> 00:04:02.030
In many ways, the Guardians of the Galaxy
sequel confirms what we already knew: Marvel

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:06.319
Studios doesn't need to rely on the traditional
superhero formula to make great movies.

00:04:06.319 --> 00:04:10.569
In fact, this film was probably one of the
least superheroic outings the studio has released

00:04:10.569 --> 00:04:11.830
since the MCU started.

00:04:11.830 --> 00:04:16.580
Sure, the gang saves the galaxy by being heroes,
but this movie had much more in common with

00:04:16.580 --> 00:04:18.709
Ghostbusters than Avengers.

00:04:18.709 --> 00:04:19.709
And that's the thing.

00:04:19.709 --> 00:04:24.650
Marvel Studios has access to nearly every
character in the Marvel Comics library.

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:28.910
And they've published a whole lot of comic
books over the years, including cowboy comics,

00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:32.790
romance comics, and even cowboy romance comics
back in the day.

00:04:32.790 --> 00:04:36.449
Marvel Studios simply needs to keep making
good movies to be successful.

00:04:36.449 --> 00:04:40.230
It doesn't matter if the characters they choose
are recognizable or not.

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:45.610
Before Guardians of the Galaxy, who'd ever
heard of Drax the Destroyer or Gamora or Ronan

00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:49.530
the Accuser, except for the geeks way in the
back of the comic shop?

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:54.280
If Marvel wants to tap new genres featuring
even more obscure characters, there's ample

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:57.360
proof that it can do so without too much risk.

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:02.270
And doing new, interesting things with big-budget
movies means audiences will have more choice.

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:03.720
That's a win.

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:04.750
Filmmakers making films

00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:09.580
Both Guardians of the Galaxy movies were very
much the product of writer-director James

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:10.580
Gunn.

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:14.350
We'll probably never know for sure how much
or how little input Marvel Studios had on

00:05:14.350 --> 00:05:20.009
the script and final cut, but these are two
very unique, very weird, very fun movies.

00:05:20.009 --> 00:05:23.690
Just as Marvel Studios can branch out into
different territory when it comes to the kinds

00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:27.560
of movies it releases, it seems as though
the studio is starting to truly trust its

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:31.460
filmmakers to deliver the goods in whatever
form they think is best.

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:35.800
Fans definitely had reason for concern when
director Edgar Wright left the Ant-Man production

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:37.980
in 2014 due to creative differences.

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:42.009
But as time goes on, that incident seems more
like a fluke than anything to truly worry

00:05:42.009 --> 00:05:43.009
about.

00:05:43.009 --> 00:05:46.590
When it comes to Marvel movies, they'll probably
be pretty good, no matter who's sitting in

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:47.860
the director's chair.

00:05:47.860 --> 00:05:48.870
Thanks for watching!

00:05:48.870 --> 00:05:51.430
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:51.430 --> 00:05:53.240
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love too.

